["2026-08-12T17:07:42.125Z",[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V1Ce38ReCyZwvGkXhLP4Cr","type":"tool_result","content":"52:3. **The base** — the exact commit id of the main state the work started from. Computed by the program, not declared (ruled 2026-08-10, replacing a caller-supplied `--base` field and its two hand-off refusals): the program runs `git merge-base HEAD origin/main`, after a fetch, in the caller's checkout — the same checkout it already reads declared content from — so every caller gets the exact right value deterministically, with no relay step to garble and no dependence on the cooperative tier being present. The result is by construction a real commit on main. Accepted residual: a caller who refreshed from main mid-task (e.g. `git pull` after starting the work) presents a too-new fork point, weakening conflict detection for that request — a blind spot the previous design shared exactly, since its wrapper derived the value from the same repo state.\n55:6. **`--agent`** — the runtime and model that produced the change (for example `claude-code/opus-5`), required and non-empty (`malformed-field`). Declared by the caller because the environment names the runtime but not the model, and the model is the half the fix ladder (the founding plan's escalation sequence for failed work — retry, stronger model, then the boss) needs (\"who last wrote this\" becomes one `git log` away). Cooperative class — recorded, not verified: the gatekeeper records what it is told and never guesses.\n56:7. **Origin** — auto-filled from the session environment; recorded as `none` when absent, never blocking (a transcript-less caller is honest, not an error). Meaningful because our agents are long-lived: the session id points at a readable transcript of intent.\n57:8. **The digest** — SHA-256 over: base id + sorted path list + each path's new bytes (deletions as a marker) + the import triple. Deliberately excluded: message, issue, mode, origin, agent, time — the digest identifies **the work**, so identical work resubmitted under different metadata still deduplicates. Canonical serialization (the built program's): NUL-framed field tags between components, so concatenation can never make two different requests read as one. Computed by the program; callers generate nothing. Scope, ruled 2026-08-10: this deduplication recognizes verbatim resubmits only — the same work against the same base. A request rebuilt after a refresh from main digests fresh; when that work is already checked in, its declared paths now match main and the answer is `unchanged-path`. Accepted residual: the situations that reach that answer are rare (a successor session with an imperfect handoff, duplicate work), and the refusal's text already names the operative fact.\n70:- The import record is read straight from history — `git log origin/main --grep \"Gatekeeper-import:\"` lists every import: what was imported, from which legacy commit, to where, when. (An `imports` subcommand that formatted this into a table was built in slice 2 and deleted by ruling 2026-08-10: the trailer is the record, the git command is the view, and a per-call scratch clone bought no guarantee. The trailer replaces the retired entry-manifest row rule; `entry-manifest.md` remains as the founding-era historical record only.)\n100:Gatekeeper-origin: <session-id> | none\n121:**States:** SCREENING (synchronous, in memory, nothing on disk) → WORKING (candidate built and checked, inside `<workspace-root>/<digest>/` — concretely `$XDG_STATE_HOME/nedschorus-gatekeeper/<digest>/`, default `~/.local/state/...` (B4a); once C2 lands this resolves in the *gatekeeper user's* environment — literal default `/home/nedschorus-gatekeeper/.local/state/nedschorus-gatekeeper/<digest>/`: outside every repository, discoverable from the digest alone, holding the candidate clone, `worker.pid`, and the resolved request record (B4c: every environment-derived field, origin foremost, is resolved at screening into that record; the worker only reads it, never re-derives) → PUSHING (atomic attempt, retry-capped) → **CHECKED-IN** or **REFUSED**. After either ending the workspace is deleted, with one exception (B4d): a refused `--no-wait` request keeps its workspace holding just the JSON refusal record; `status` returns it once, then sweeps. Expiry (ruled 2026-08-10): any gatekeeper invocation first sweeps refusal records older than 30 days — opportunistic, no daemon; a caller whose record aged out resubmits, the same recovery as every lost reason. Named residual (accepted): a caller crashing between sweep and read loses the reason — rare, recoverable by resubmit. Durable traces: a checked-in request is its commit on main; a refused waiting request deliberately leaves nothing.\n143:- **The Unix-user boundary (C2) — where the enforcement actually lives:** GitHub restricts accounts, not processes, and on one machine every process of one Unix user reads the same credential files. So the main-capable credential is owned by a **dedicated Unix user**, unreadable by agent sessions, and agents invoke the program through a sudoers rule scoped to exactly it. That is the step at which \"agents never push\" becomes impossible rather than instructed; it also makes the gatekeeper literally an invocable service any agent on the box can call. The sudoers rule names a **root-owned copy of the program outside every checkout** (ruled 2026-08-10) — a rule pointing into a checkout would privilege whatever bytes an agent last wrote there. The copy keeps itself current from main automatically (no manual deploy step; a checked-in fix is live on the next call), which is safe because the gatekeeper's source joins the instruction-file class: changes reach main only with walked-approval evidence, enforced by the review-evidence check (slice 6). Activating the privileged lane therefore waits on slice 6. Working path: `/usr/local/lib/nedschorus-gatekeeper/git-gatekeeper.py`. Staleness of the deployed copy is fail-safe — a stale copy enforces the old contract and can never be made to run agent-written bytes; ownership, not freshness, carries the security guarantee, so a lag in self-update costs availability, never safety. Standing principle, project-wide: a deployed copy of anything keeps itself current from its source automatically rather than relying on a remembered deploy step.\n146:- **Break-glass (C5) — an unlockable credential, never a standing ungated agent:** gate defects are recovered from the gate's own history — the program stays **one standard-library-only file** precisely so any historical version is directly runnable; a check-in the gate wrongly refuses uses a sudoers entry requiring the user's password, approved in the moment; credential expiry and protection misconfiguration are org-owner territory, the user's alone.\n162:| Dedicated gatekeeper identity | Two agents, one machine; the procedural gap is audit-detected (that audit itself deleted 2026-08-10) | Original trigger: \"The audit fires, or the boss admits it early\" — the second arm fired: **admitted 2026-08-09**, § The credential and enforcement |\n174:Build sequencing is owned by `docs/issues/3-git-gatekeeper-build-slice-plan.md` (five slices, with the test↔slice map; this section's original one-task framing is superseded by it, ruled 2026-08-10). What remains here is the contract's acceptance-test index:","is_error":false}]]
